                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 811,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 908,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Splits a matrix into Q, whose columns are perpendicular directions of length 1, and R, a triangle: the input equals Q x R.\n\nQ is the useful half on its own: it turns a set of arbitrary directions (the columns of the input) into a set of mutually perpendicular ones spanning the same space - the first column of Q points along the first column of the input, the second is the part of the second column at right angles to the first, and so on. R records how to mix Q's columns back into the input, and is zero below its diagonal because each input column only needs the Q columns up to its own. It is also the stable way least-squares problems are solved.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.qr\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe matrix (a list of lists or a NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor), rows x columns. Receiving it decomposes and sends.\n\nmode (menu):\nreduced (the default): for a 4 x 3 input, Q is 4 x 3 and R 3 x 3.\ncomplete: Q is square, 4 x 4 (one more perpendicular direction), and R 4 x 3 with a row of zeros.\nr: R only - Q is sent as an EMPTY tensor, which the pictures here cannot draw.\nChanging it does not send by itself; the next matrix uses it (here the matrix is re-sent ten times a second, so a change shows at once).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nQ tensor out:\nQ, columns of length 1 at right angles to each other. Sent second.\n\nR tensor out:\nR, upper triangular. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_matrix sends a 4 x 3 matrix. Q (blue above 0, red below) has the same shape; R's zeros below the diagonal show as the neutral colour. The bottom picture is Q times R from np.matmul (R arrives first and is stored, then Q triggers): the input again. Choose 'complete' and Q gains a fourth column and R a zero row; the product is unchanged.\nSigns are free: a column of Q and the matching row of R can both flip and the product stays the same.\n\nRELATED:\nt.linalg.svd breaks a matrix into patterns and their strengths. t.linalg.eig finds the directions a square matrix only stretches. np.linalg.solve solves the equations that qr is often used for. np.matmul multiplies the pieces back together.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 892,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Splits a matrix into Q, whose columns are perpendicular directions of length 1, and R, a triangle: the input equals Q x R.\n\nQ is the useful half on its own: it turns a set of arbitrary directions (the columns of the input) into a set of mutually perpendicular ones spanning the same space - the first column of Q points along the first column of the input, the second is the part of the second column at right angles to the first, and so on. R records how to mix Q's columns back into the input, and is zero below its diagonal because each input column only needs the Q columns up to its own. It is also the stable way least-squares problems are solved.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.linalg.qr\n(no arguments)\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe matrix (a list of lists or a NumPy array is turned into a float32 tensor), rows x columns. Receiving it decomposes and sends.\n\nmode (menu):\nreduced (the default): for a 4 x 3 input, Q is 4 x 3 and R 3 x 3.\ncomplete: Q is square, 4 x 4 (one more perpendicular direction), and R 4 x 3 with a row of zeros.\nr: R only - Q is sent as an EMPTY tensor, which the pictures here cannot draw.\nChanging it does not send by itself; the next matrix uses it (here the matrix is re-sent ten times a second, so a change shows at once).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nQ tensor out:\nQ, columns of length 1 at right angles to each other. Sent second.\n\nR tensor out:\nR, upper triangular. Sent first.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nmake_matrix sends a 4 x 3 matrix. Q (blue above 0, red below) has the same shape; R's zeros below the diagonal show as the neutral colour. The bottom picture is Q times R from np.matmul (R arrives first and is stored, then Q triggers): the input again. Choose 'complete' and Q gains a fourth column and R a zero row; the product is unchanged.\nSigns are free: a column of Q and the matching row of R can both flip and the product stays the same.\n\nRELATED:\nt.linalg.svd breaks a matrix into patterns and their strengths. t.linalg.eig finds the directions a square matrix only stretches. np.linalg.solve solves the equations that qr is often used for. np.matmul multiplies the pieces back together."
                },
